  "text": "Okay this one has a man so awful that Scripture literally says his name means fool.\n\nNabal. Three thousand sheep, a thousand goats, shearing season, rolling in it. And David, before he's king, has been out in the wilderness guarding this man's shepherds. For free. The servants say it themselves: they were a wall unto us, night and day.\n\nSo David sends ten men to ask, politely, for provisions.\n\nAnd Nabal goes, \"Who is David? Who is the son of Jesse?\" Then, \"Shall I take my bread and my water and my meat, and give it to men I know not whence they be?\"\n\nSir. You know exactly who he is.\n\nDavid's reply is two words. \"Gird ye on every man his sword.\" Four hundred men. He is riding out to erase an entire household over a dinner request.\n\nNow. A servant runs to Nabal's wife, because the staff know you cannot talk to this man.\n\nEnter Abigail. And she does not panic, she does inventory. Two hundred loaves. Two skins of wine. Five dressed sheep. Roasted grain. A hundred raisin cakes. Two hundred fig cakes. Loaded on donkeys, sent ahead.\n\nAnd she tells her husband nothing.\n\nShe rides straight at four hundred armed men, gets off the donkey, falls on her face, and takes the blame herself. Then she tells David: do not let this blood be on you, because God is going to build you a sure house, and you do not want this on the record.\n\nAnd David says, \"Blessed be thy advice, and blessed be thou, which hast kept me this day from coming to shed blood.\"\n\nShe talked a future king out of a massacre. With groceries and a speech.",
